Working directory: <workspace> (Hono repo). Only touch the two files listed below. Do NOT run git add/stage/commit; leave changes unstaged.

GOAL: Fix the Set-Cookie merge bug in the `Context.res` setter and add/update tests.

CURRENT BUG — src/context.ts lines 414-434 (setter):
```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type') {
          continue
        }
        if (k === 'set-cookie') {
          const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
          _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
          for (const cookie of cookies) {
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        } else {
          _res.headers.set(k, v)
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```
The `_res.headers.delete('set-cookie')` wipes Set-Cookie headers that the new Response (`_res`) already carries. When middleware has set a cookie on `c.res` and a handler returns a Response with its own Set-Cookie, both must survive. Also verified: `Headers.entries()` yields each `set-cookie` value as a separate entry (Node 24), so merging old cookies inside the `entries()` loop would append them once per entry and create duplicates.

REQUIRED FIX (src/context.ts): In the setter, skip BOTH `content-type` and `set-cookie` inside the `entries()` loop, then after the loop append every old cookie exactly once via `this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()` without deleting the new response's cookies. Existing Set-Cookie headers on `_res` are preserved first, then the old `#res` cookies are appended. Suggested shape:

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
          continue
        }
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
      for (const cookie of this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()) {
        _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```
Keep behavior for all other headers unchanged (new response headers win except set-cookie merge; content-type from new response wins).

TESTS — src/context.test.ts, inside `describe('Context header', ...)`:
1. UPDATE the existing test `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to ` + '`c.res`'` (currently around lines 430-445). It encodes the old buggy behavior (`expect(...getSetCookie()).toEqual(cookies)` after reassignment, expecting the new response's cookie to be dropped). Change its expectations so all cookies survive: after reassigning `newResponse` carrying `foo3=bar3; Path=/`, the result must contain the two original cookies plus `foo3=bar3; Path=/` — length 3 and `toEqual([...newCookies, ...cookies])` (new-response cookies first, then previously stored cookies). Keep the test name meaningful.
2. ADD a new regression test (place it right after that test) explicitly modeling middleware + handler, e.g.:

```ts
  it('Should keep both set-cookie headers when middleware and handler each set one', () => {
    const middlewareCookie = 'middleware=1; Path=/'
    c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', middlewareCookie)

    const handlerCookie = 'handler=1; Path=/'
    const handlerResponse = new Response('ok', {
      headers: { 'set-cookie': handlerCookie },
    })
    c.res = handlerResponse

    const cookies = c.res.headers.getSetCookie()
    expect(cookies.length).toBe(2)
    expect(cookies).toContain(middlewareCookie)
    expect(cookies).toContain(handlerCookie)
  })
```

ACCEPTANCE:
- `pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts` passes (run it yourself to confirm).
- No unrelated changes, no formatting churn beyond these edits.
- Report the exact diff of both files plus the test command output summary, and confirm the new regression test fails on the pre-fix code conceptually (the delete line is the cause).

